 {"id":"CF-068","title":"P3 — project-node.sh proves bus identity by FILE EXISTENCE: a 0-byte mode-600 crier.json passes crier_identity, so a node with no bus identity reports PASS on the layer that exists to catch exactly that","status":"pending","priority":"P3","complexity":3,"reasoning":"Measured 2026-11-03 by the code-factory releng-QA lane at e46b870 (fresh clone of the published tip, clean porcelain). scripts/project-node.sh:137 is `if [ -f \"$STATE/crier.json\" ]`; on that test alone it prints `ok \"crier_identity\" \"$STATE/crier.json\"` (:138), then :139 checks the file mode (600) and nothing else. No JSON parse, no required-key check, no size check: the layer named `crier_identity` in its own failure text (\"a node without an identity is invisible\") proves only that a path exists. The probe field is the measurement: a 0-byte ~/.node/crier.json with mode 600 prints `PASS  crier_identity` and `PASS  crier_mode — 600`, while the same tree with the file absent prints `FAIL  crier_identity — absent — stage a bundle and re-run` (one more FAIL, 4 vs 3). So an empty, truncated, or fabricated bundle is indistinguishable from a real one on this layer, and layer 90 admits the node on that basis (the manifest at :183 records only bus.base, never an agent id, so nothing downstream re-checks it either). docs/OPERATIONS.md:40 states the secure bundle workflow is a requirement, not a supplied operation, and warns \"Do not hand-create a fake identity\" — the current check certifies precisely that fake. docs/THREAT-MODEL.md:44 already records that only the mode is checked. Distinct from CF-051/CF-056 (node-create.sh stages) and from the ACCEPTANCE-PLAN's CF-111 manifest-schema gap: this is the layer-30 admission check itself certifying content it never opens.","acceptance_criteria":"0. Reproduce on the current tree (from the repo root; prints `PASS  crier_identity` and `PASS  crier_mode` for a 0-byte bundle today, and `FAIL  crier_identity — absent` for the control).\n1. `./scripts/project-node.sh --check` on a 0-byte or unparseable `$HOME/.node/crier.json` FAILs `crier_identity` with a reason that names the defect (empty / not JSON), and the FAIL is counted in `NODE: FAIL (n)`. A valid bundle fixture still PASSes both `crier_identity` and `crier_mode`.\n2. The required content is the one specs/NODE.md already names for bus identity (agent id and the bus base at minimum) and the spec text and the check ship in the same commit — no new undocumented keys.\n3. A mode-644 valid bundle still FAILs `crier_mode` (the mode test must survive the content test), both directions asserted in the same run.\n4. tests/selftest.sh gains a fixture for this: the pre-fix control (current script, 0-byte bundle) shows `PASS  crier_identity`, the post-fix run shows `FAIL`, and `bash tests/selftest.sh` prints SELFTEST: PASS at the fixed tip.\n5. AGENTS.md rule 2 (every check must be able to fail) is named in the commit message, because this is a gate-integrity change, not a new feature.","created_at":"2026-11-03T14:23:00Z","updated_at":"2026-11-03T14:23:00Z","probe":"h=$(mktemp -d); mkdir -p \"$h/.node\"; : > \"$h/.node/crier.json\"; chmod 600 \"$h/.node/crier.json\"; echo \"--- empty bundle ---\"; HOME=\"$h\" CRIER_URL=http://127.0.0.1:9 ./scripts/project-node.sh --check --project demo 2>&1 | grep -E 'crier_identity|crier_mode'; echo \"--- absent bundle ---\"; h2=$(mktemp -d); HOME=\"$h2\" CRIER_URL=http://127.0.0.1:9 ./scripts/project-node.sh --check --project demo 2>&1 | grep -E 'crier_identity|crier_mode'; rm -rf \"$h\" \"$h2\""}
 {"id":"CF-069","title":"P4 — the selftest board-validation arm is fail-open: when its python3 emits nothing, the comparison is an empty = empty and the run prints 'PASS  / rows parse' with no counts","status":"pending","priority":"P4","complexity":2,"reasoning":"Measured 2026-11-03 by the code-factory releng-QA lane at e46b870. tests/selftest.sh:162-175 runs `read -r good n < <(python3 - \"$b\" <<'PY' ... print(good, len(rows)) PY)` and then `[ \"$good\" = \"$n\" ] && say \"  PASS  $good/$n rows parse\"`. Both variables are assigned by the process substitution; if that substitution produces no output — python3 absent, python3 exiting nonzero before the print, the here-document failing to parse, or the file being unreadable — the read fails and both names stay UNSET, so the comparison is `\"\" = \"\"`, which is true: the arm prints `PASS  / rows parse` and adds nothing to the failure counter. The probe field is the measurement: with a PATH-local python3 that exits 1 silently, the suite prints `PASS  / rows parse` (empty counts) while nine other sections correctly report FAIL. The arm therefore cannot fail in the one state where it proves nothing, which is the class AGENTS.md rule 2 forbids; a broken or shadowed interpreter silently disables the only check that the append-only board parses. Distinct from CF-048 (an unterminated final line is skipped by the parser, so a truncated board passes): that defect is in row selection while the arm still runs; this one is the arm itself dying green. Coverage gap in the suite: no fixture runs this arm with a dead interpreter or an empty board file, so nothing in the repo currently notices.","acceptance_criteria":"0. Reproduce on the current tree (from the repo root; prints `PASS  / rows parse` with empty counts today).\n1. The arm initializes its counters to a sentinel before the read and FAILs unless the helper printed exactly two integers with good == n and n > 0; a helper that exits nonzero, prints nothing, or prints malformed output FAILs with the reason named (interpreter error vs unparsable output).\n2. A board file with zero rows is a named FAIL (or an explicit, named skip that still exits 0 only when the board is deliberately absent), never `PASS  / rows parse` with empty counts.\n3. Positive control in the same run: the real board still prints its literal counts (`n/ n rows parse` with n equal to `wc -l` of tasks.jsonl) and the suite prints SELFTEST: PASS at the fixed tip; a pre-fix control with the dead-interpreter shim shows the green-with-empty-counts result the new fixture rejects.\n4. tests/selftest.sh covers all three states (real interpreter, exit-nonzero stub, interpreter absent from PATH) and the stub/absent states FAIL with a named row rather than a shell error containing the substring FAIL.","created_at":"2026-11-03T14:23:00Z","updated_at":"2026-11-03T14:23:00Z","probe":"d=$(mktemp -d); mkdir -p \"$d/bin\"; printf '#!/bin/sh\\nexit 1\\n' > \"$d/bin/python3\"; chmod +x \"$d/bin/python3\"; PATH=\"$d/bin:$PATH\" bash tests/selftest.sh 2>&1 | grep -E 'rows parse'; rm -rf \"$d\""}
+{"id":"CF-070","title":"P3 — the inference-key gate proves a KEY NAME is assigned, not that a key exists: an empty `DEMO_API_KEY=` in ~/.hermes/.env prints PASS inference_key","status":"pending","priority":"P3","complexity":2,"reasoning":"Measured 2026-11-03 by the code-factory releng-QA lane at 8889cad. scripts/harness/install-hermes.sh:76-80 decides the layer with `if [ -f \"$HOME/.hermes/.env\" ] && grep -qE '^[A-Z_]+_API_KEY=' \"$HOME/.hermes/.env\"` and then prints `ok \"inference_key\" \"a provider key is present in ~/.hermes/.env (value not read)\"`. The regex asserts that SOME line assigns a variable whose name ends in _API_KEY; it says nothing about the value. Measured two-arm: a .env containing exactly `DEMO_API_KEY=` (empty value) prints `PASS  inference_key — a provider key is present`, while a .env holding the same line commented out prints `FAIL  inference_key — no provider key — a turn dies at 'No inference provider configured'`. So the check separates 'no assignment' from 'assignment', not 'no key' from 'key'. Consequence inside the shipped pipeline: scripts/project-node.sh layer 20 runs this adapter, echoes its rows and adds its `  FAIL` rows to the parent counter (CF-055, verified this tick: 5 printed FAIL rows -> NODE: FAIL (5)); a keyless-but-assigned .env therefore contributes zero failures, the node passes the layering, and --apply writes .node/node.json (:176-189) for a node whose every turn dies at the exact 'No inference provider configured' error the row exists to prevent. The same weakness accepts a whitespace-only value and a value that is a placeholder like `x`; the check cannot distinguish them because it never reads the value. Distinct from CF-002 (a P0 about the credential's RESTRICTED scope and fail-closed revoke, not about the presence check) and from CF-068 (bus identity proven by file existence in project-node.sh:137, a different artifact and layer). Coverage gap: no section of tests/selftest.sh exercises the adapter's inference_key arm at all, so both the empty-value and the comment-only forms are untested.","acceptance_criteria":"0. Reproduce on the current tree (from the repo root; prints `PASS  inference_key — a provider key is present in ~/.hermes/.env (value not read)` for a .env whose only content is `DEMO_API_KEY=`, and exits 0 today).\n1. An assignment with an empty or whitespace-only value is a named FAIL: the row reads `FAIL  inference_key — <VARNAME> is assigned but has no value (a turn dies at 'No inference provider configured')`.\n2. Positive control in the same run: a .env whose assignment has a non-empty value still prints PASS, and the value itself is never printed or logged (only the variable name and a presence verdict).\n3. A .env with no matching assignment at all keeps the current FAIL wording, and the three arms (empty value, non-empty value, no assignment) are covered by a fixture in tests/selftest.sh that asserts each named row rather than a bare substring.\n4. `bash tests/selftest.sh` prints SELFTEST: PASS at the fixed tip.","probe":"d=$(mktemp -d); mkdir -p \"$d/h/.hermes\"; printf 'DEMO_API_KEY=\\n' > \"$d/h/.hermes/.env\"; HOME=\"$d/h\" HERMES_PYTHON_BIN=${HERMES_PYTHON_BIN:-/usr/bin/python3.12} bash scripts/harness/install-hermes.sh --check 2>&1 | grep 'inference_key'","created_at":"2026-11-03T14:45:00Z","updated_at":"2026-11-03T14:45:00Z"}
+{"id":"CF-071","title":"P3 — the public-bind auth gate is a substring grep: a COMMENTED-OUT `basic_auth:` in config.yaml satisfies it and --apply proceeds to start a 0.0.0.0 gateway","status":"pending","priority":"P3","complexity":2,"reasoning":"Measured 2026-11-03 by the code-factory releng-QA lane at 8889cad. scripts/harness/install-hermes.sh:10-11 states the guarantee — 'A public bind ALWAYS requires auth ... so --apply refuses to start a gateway without dashboard.basic_auth configured' — and :94 implements it as `if grep -q 'basic_auth' \"$HOME/.hermes/config.yaml\"`. A substring match cannot tell a configured block from a commented-out stub: a config whose only occurrence is `# dashboard:` / `#   basic_auth: user:pass` passes the gate. Measured two-arm with a PATH-local `hermes` shim (so nothing is installed) and one throwaway HOME per arm: arm A (commented stub) reaches the launch path and prints `FAIL  gateway_start — -> 000000 (see ~/.node-serve-10800.log)` — i.e. it ran `nohup hermes serve --host 0.0.0.0 --port 10800 --skip-build`; arm B (empty config.yaml) prints `FAIL  gateway_start — refused: a public bind needs dashboard.basic_auth in ~/.hermes/config.yaml`. The difference between the two arms is exactly the gate's decision, so the gate's whole contribution is 'the string basic_auth exists somewhere in this file'. The same weakness accepts `basic_auth:` with no username/password (an empty credential block) and a block nested under the wrong key. Blast radius: the node's gateway binds all interfaces over the tailnet; scripts/verify-node.sh step 3 is the only check on the other side and it probes an HTTP path, not the config, so a commented-out stub produces a node that is open to every tailnet peer while both shipped checks call the layer satisfied. Distinct from CF-068 (artifact existence in project-node.sh:137) and from CF-070 (the inference_key presence regex): this is a security gate decided by substring rather than by a parsed, non-empty value. Coverage gap: no selftest section exercises the auth-gate branch at all (the suite never sets a config.yaml), so the commented and empty-value forms are untested.","acceptance_criteria":"0. Reproduce on the current tree (from the repo root; a config.yaml whose only `basic_auth` occurrence is inside a comment reaches the launch path and prints `FAIL  gateway_start — -> 000000 …` today).\n1. A config.yaml where `basic_auth` appears only inside a comment, or where the block exists with an empty username or password, is REFUSED with the same wording as an absent config (`refused: a public bind needs dashboard.basic_auth …`) and the refusal names the reason (commented / empty value / block absent).\n2. Positive control in the same run: a config.yaml with a real non-empty basic_auth block proceeds past the gate (reaches the launch path) so the new check is not simply always-refuse.\n3. The decision reads the parsed value (YAML parse of the dashboard/auth block, or the gateway's own config loader) and never a bare `grep -q`; the check still runs before any `hermes serve` invocation.\n4. tests/selftest.sh covers the three arms (commented, empty-value, real value) with named rows, and `bash tests/selftest.sh` prints SELFTEST: PASS at the fixed tip.","probe":"d=$(mktemp -d); mkdir -p \"$d/bin\" \"$d/h/.hermes\"; printf '#!/bin/sh\\necho \"hermes 0.0.0-shim\"\\n' > \"$d/bin/hermes\"; chmod +x \"$d/bin/hermes\"; printf '# dashboard:\\n#   basic_auth: user:pass\\n' > \"$d/h/.hermes/config.yaml\"; printf 'DEMO_API_KEY=x\\n' > \"$d/h/.hermes/.env\"; HOME=\"$d/h\" PATH=\"$d/bin:$PATH\" HERMES_PYTHON_BIN=${HERMES_PYTHON_BIN:-/usr/bin/python3.12} bash scripts/harness/install-hermes.sh --apply 2>&1 | grep -E 'gateway_start|HERMES:'","created_at":"2026-11-03T14:46:00Z","updated_at":"2026-11-03T14:46:00Z"}
+{"id":"CF-072","title":"P4 — the CF-037 '-> 000000' deformation survives in three scripts the fix did not sweep: install-hermes.sh:68,97 and verify-node.sh:42,53 still append a second 000 to curl's own status line","status":"pending","priority":"P4","complexity":2,"reasoning":"Measured 2026-11-03 by the code-factory releng-QA lane at 8889cad. CF-037 (complete) closed 'project-node preflight prints -> 000000 for an unreachable URL and hides the real cause'; the fix landed only at scripts/project-node.sh:95, which now captures the status with `if ! c=$(curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}' …); then c=000; fi`. The identical construct is still shipped in three other places: scripts/verify-node.sh:42 and :53, and scripts/harness/install-hermes.sh:68 and :97 — each of them `code=$(curl -s -o … -w '%{http_code}' --max-time N \"$url\" 2>/dev/null || echo 000)`. curl already prints 000 and exits 7 when it cannot connect, so the `|| echo 000` appends a second one and the variable becomes a two-line '000\\n000'; the FAIL row then reads `-> 000000` (a six-digit token that no status code can match) exactly as CF-037 described. Measured: with a PATH-local `ss` shim reporting `:10800` as listening while nothing listens, install-hermes.sh --check prints `FAIL  status_probe — /api/status -> 000000`. The comparison against 200 still fails, so this is not a false PASS — it is the diagnostic regression CF-037 was filed for, left in three scripts, and it also reaches the operator through verify-node.sh, whose step-2 row is the one that tells them whether the gateway is up or whether they probed a closed port. Related defect in the same block: verify-node.sh:42 writes the status body to the fixed path /tmp/node-status.json, which every lane and every concurrent run on the host shares (the CF-056 class), so :45 can grep a body another run wrote.","acceptance_criteria":"0. Reproduce on the current tree (from the repo root; prints `FAIL  status_probe — /api/status -> 000000` today).\n1. All four sites print exactly one three-digit code (`-> 000`) and capture curl's exit status separately, using the project-node.sh:95 shape; a grep for `|| echo 000` across scripts/ returns no hit.\n2. verify-node.sh writes its status body to a per-run `mktemp` path (removed on exit), not /tmp/node-status.json, and the auth_gate grep reads that path.\n3. A fixture in tests/selftest.sh asserts the three-digit shape for the closed-port case in install-hermes.sh and for verify-node.sh (stubbed `bunker` + closed port give `-> 000`), asserting the named row shape and explicitly rejecting the substring '000000'.\n4. `bash tests/selftest.sh` prints SELFTEST: PASS at the fixed tip.","probe":"d=$(mktemp -d); mkdir -p \"$d/bin\" \"$d/h/.hermes\"; printf '#!/bin/sh\\necho \"LISTEN 0 128 0.0.0.0:10800 0.0.0.0:*\"\\n' > \"$d/bin/ss\"; chmod +x \"$d/bin/ss\"; printf 'DEMO_API_KEY=x\\n' > \"$d/h/.hermes/.env\"; HOME=\"$d/h\" PATH=\"$d/bin:$PATH\" HERMES_PYTHON_BIN=${HERMES_PYTHON_BIN:-/usr/bin/python3.12} bash scripts/harness/install-hermes.sh --check 2>&1 | grep 'status_probe'","created_at":"2026-11-03T14:47:00Z","updated_at":"2026-11-03T14:47:00Z"}
